1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,822
Entities: one record, its whole history.

2
00:00:04,022 --> 00:00:08,326
One antibody, trastuzumab, lives in
five spreadsheets and a notebook.

3
00:00:08,326 --> 00:00:13,741
Each copy has its own name, and
its own version of the sequence.

4
00:00:13,741 --> 00:00:17,804
The same molecule, six
versions of the truth.

5
00:00:19,904 --> 00:00:24,948
In Iki, it’s one entity: a typed
record, defined by a plugin.

6
00:00:24,948 --> 00:00:29,993
The antibody plugin defines the type:
its chains, format, isotype and target.

7
00:00:31,193 --> 00:00:35,496
Iki knows what makes two
antibodies the same: their chains.

8
00:00:35,496 --> 00:00:41,652
Register the same chains again, and
Iki finds ABY-000001, not a duplicate.

9
00:00:42,852 --> 00:00:48,267
One registry entry, one ID. Each
project keeps its own record of it.

10
00:00:49,467 --> 00:00:54,881
Every change is a new revision: who,
when, how, and any reason given.

11
00:00:54,881 --> 00:00:58,444
Revisions can’t be changed
or removed, by anyone.

12
00:00:59,644 --> 00:01:05,059
Entities link to everything made from
them, so you can always trace back.

13
00:01:05,059 --> 00:01:10,474
A lot’s release takes QA’s signature:
a fresh sign-in, tied to that revision.

14
00:01:11,674 --> 00:01:15,978
Entities are what everything
in Iki works on. Next: actions.
